\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Up Late Amid Broadly Weak U.S. Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger late Tuesday in Asia amid broad U.S. dollar weakness sparked by renewed fears of further global terrorism after an Israeli missile attack Monday killed Palestinian Hamas founder Ahmed Yassin.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were also slightly higher at the end of the session, in line with U.S. Treasurys, reflecting the heightened fears of terrorism.  \par \par \uc2 At 0750 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.6878, down from S$1.6927 late Monday in Asia. It traded in the S$1.6874-S$1.6996 range intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The missile attack, which was condemned by governments in the Arab world, Europe and Japan, reignited geopolitical worries, prompting some players to push the U.S. dollar down as low as S$1.6877 in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later rebounded slightly on light buybacks spurred by jitters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar-\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar pair.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said market participants were wary of driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down toward S$1.6860, the three-week low set last week where the MAS was rumored to have called banks to ask about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the possibility of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, the U.S. dollar edged back down against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session, hitting a fresh intraday low of S$1.6874 on continuing fears of more terrorism globally.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to hold ground in the high S$1.6800s, technical analysis suggests the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term corrective rebound may have ended at S$1.6960 Monday, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 But UOB added the U.S. dollar's downward momentum against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't strong and the S$1.6840 support will likely be difficult to break. It also recommended selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.6910 and placing a stop-loss order above S$1.6940 as a short-term trading strategy. [ 23-03-04 0834GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly marginally higher as they tracked U.S. Treasurys on heightened concerns about global terrorism after Monday's assassination of the Hamas founder.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds, however, showed little reaction to the release of Singapore's February consumer price index, which rose 1.5% on year, at the high end of expectations of a 1.0%-1.5% increase.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said although theoretically bonds should have reacted to the bigger-than-expected increase, in practice Singapore bonds tend to take their cue from U.S. Treasurys instead.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the two-year Singapore government securities was 0.92%, down four basis points from Monday's close. At the longer end of the curve, the yield on 10-year bonds was flat at 3.02%, while the 15-year yield slipped two basis points to 3.44%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps also were mostly slightly lower. The two-year swap offer was at 1.31%, against 1.35% late Monday, while the five-year offer was at 2.31%, versus 2.34%. The 10-year swap offer fell one basis point to 3.33%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 202 basis points from 199 points late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight rate fell to 0.1875% from 0.3125%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Shishir Mudgil [ 23-03-04 0848GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040323e03n000cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
