\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late On US Dlr Weakness Versus Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar extended its rally against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third straight session late in Asia Wednesday amid pronounced U.S. dollar weakness against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Singapore government bond prices were slightly higher, following a rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight after the U.S. Federal Reserve suggested it would remain very patient before raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6966, down from S$1.7055 late in Asia Tuesday. It traded a S$1.6965-S$1.7030 range intraday.  \par \par \uc2 With the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extending its overnight fall against the yen to the low Y108s, non-Singaporean players sold the U.S. unit against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dragging the U.S. dollar down.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger-than-expected Singapore trade data during the session also weighed on the U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar pair. The city-state's non-oil domestic exports rose 25.7% on year in February, exceeding the top range of economists' expectations due to an impressive performance by the pharmaceutical and petrochemical segments.  \par \par \uc2 The city-state's improving economic fundamentals could continue to underpin the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said, and may give the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem excessive appreciation of the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 Based on technical analysis, the pair could fall further in coming days. The pair would trigger a bearish double-top pattern on the daily charts if it trades below S$1.7005 later in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The S$1.7005 level is the March 9 low between the two tops - the March 4 high of S$1.7193 and the March 12 high of S$1.7180 - and technical analysis suggests a U.S. dollar close below that level would imply a decline to at least S$1.6823.  \par \par \uc2 Also pressuring the U.S. unit in the near term is a sell signal on the moving average convergence divergence, and the break of the 100-day moving average support late Tuesday at S$1.7070. [ 17-03-04 0824GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Near the end of the session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was 0.92% from 0.94% late Tuesday. At the longer end of the curve, the yield on 10-year bonds fell to 2.87% from 2.93%, and the 15-year yield was 3.34% versus 3.40% previously.  \par \par \uc2 DBS Bank said in a commentary early in the session that there was limited downside in government bond yields and, citing signs of recovery in the Singapore economy, forecast the 10-year bond yield would likely trend above the 3% mark in coming weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were lower in line with local bond yields. The two-year swap offer was 1.29% from 1.33% late Tuesday, while the five-year offer was 2.20% versus 2.28%. The 10-year swap offer was 3.20%, down from 3.28%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 191 basis points from 195 basis points late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight rate rose to 0.3750% from 0.1875%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 