\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Singapore's economic outlook brightens in 2004.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacqueline Wong  \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 10:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 26 (Reuters) - Singapore got a surprising boost from its rapidly growing pharmaceuticals sector in January, and the government raised its growth forecast for 2004, suggesting the economy is set for its best performance in four years.  \par \par \uc2 With drug and electronics production gathering pace due to growing demand from global markets, the government said on Thursday growth this year was likely to be around 3.5 to 5.5 percent, up from its previous forecast of 3.0 to 5.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore's gross domestic product (GDP) grew at an 11 percent annualised rate in the three months through December after adjustment for seasonal patterns, the government said, topping market expectations for growth of about nine percent.  \par \par \uc2 This brought 2003 growth to 1.1 percent, revised up from an estimate issued last month of 0.8 percent but only half of the 2.2 percent growth seen in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "The external sector has helped lift the figures in the final sprint. But it's still an uneven pace, led primarily by the external sector," said Song Seng Wun, an economist at G.K. Goh.  \par \par \uc2 Unemployment has come off 17-year highs, but the city state is grappling with structural pressures and factories face intense competition from China and India.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters say Singapore should hit the top of the government's new range with growth of about 5.2 percent this year as busy factories drive the recovery.  \par \par \uc2 From U.S hard disk drive maker Seagate Technology to the world's largest drug manufacturer, Pfizer Inc, factories are ramping up production.  \par \par \uc2 "The first quarter will be much more important in signalling the underlying trend. On the whole, we are still quite bullish," said Lian Chia-liang, an economist at JP Morgan Chase. He expects first-quarter annualised growth of six percent.  \par \par \uc2 BIRD FLU RISK  \par \par \uc2 But the Ministry of Trade and Industry tempered the optimism, pointing to risks posed by outbreaks of bird flu sweeping parts of Asia, political uncertainty in the region and a weak U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Friedrich Wu, director, economics division of the ministry, told a news conference that a sharp correction in the U.S. dollar could trigger a sell-off of U.S. dollar assets and a rise in U.S. interest rates, which would drag down the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe that Asian central banks are likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to prevent this from occurring as their economies remain highly dependent on U.S. demand," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the U.S. dollar continued to slide against the Singapore dollar, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) official declined comment.  \par \par \uc2 "We do not comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and reiterate we maintain the exchange rate against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and there is no change in that regime," Khor Hoe Ee, an assistant managing director at the MAS said.  \par \par \uc2 The growth data helped the Singapore dollar withstand a broad-based rally in the U.S. dollar on Thursday, but dealers said it would not entirely shield the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Sing dollar traded around 1.6955 per U.S. dollar in late afternoon, off late Wednesday's 1.6900 level but above three-week lows around 1.7020 per dollar hit on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 BOUNCING FROM SARS  \par \par \uc2 The December quarter marked a slowdown from brisk third quarter annualised growth of 16.1 percent, the fastest since 1997, as Singapore rebounded from a SARS slump that sent its $90 billion economy into its worst ever quarterly contraction.  \par \par \uc2 Manufacturing output rose a surprising 18.1 percent in January from December, after adjustment for seasonal patterns, as drug makers offset slower factory activity elsewhere due to the Lunar New Year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said leading indicators such as the U.S. semiconductor book-to-bill ratio signal a positive trend.  \par \par \uc2 The stronger outlook could pave the way for Goh Chok Tong, prime minister since 1990, to hand power to Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong.  \par \par \uc2 Goh has said he would be comfortable making way for Lee - son of founding prime minister Lee Kuan Yew - if the economy were growing at a full-year rate of three to four percent.  \par \par \uc2 "You might as well have early elections while the economy is still running hot," said Lian of JP Morgan, who saw a snap poll in the third quarter as a possibility. (Additional reporting by Jennifer Tan).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
