\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down Late As Players Jump Back Into US Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 09:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was weaker late in Asia Thursday as various participants jumped back into long U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar positions after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong rally in New York Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0730 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6826, up sharply from S$1.6727 Wednesday. It traded a range of S$1.6780-S$1.6833 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's rally against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from new lows overnight spooked Singapore banks and U.S. investment houses into buying back the U.S. unit against its Singaporean counterpart just one day after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen as low as the psychologically key S$1.6700 level in Asia Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The turnaround in the U.S. unit's fortunes overnight stemmed from heavy buying by investors, especially hedge funds, to unwind huge short U.S. dollar positions against the euro, the sterling pound and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for profit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit climbed against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the session on the buying by domestic and U.S. investment banks, but it soon ran into thick offers from Singapore companies just above the psychologically important S$1.6800 level.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the session, however, the U.S. dollar finally overcame the persistent selling from those firms to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, extending its gains to the low S$1.6800s.  \par \par \uc2 With the U.S. unit having found solid support at S$1.6770-S$1.6780, it could rise further toward its key psychological resistance of S$1.6900 in the coming sessions, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But technical analysis still suggests the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar is in a short-term downtrend. The U.S. unit hasn't yet hit S$1.6638, the minimum target of a bearish right triangle triggered earlier this month. Also, the three-point downtrend line from the end of January that comes in today at S$1.6891 (and falling S$0.0012/day) is intact.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds fell on supply-related concerns, following news that U.S. mortgage finance agency Fannie Mae was selling a further S$150 million of its Singapore-dollar bond due 2009 and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore will sell S$1.2 billion of an existing government bond due 2011 next week.  \par \par \uc2 There was also lingering interest to hedge a recent Housing Development Board issue, which pushed interest rate swaps higher, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight, prices of U.S. Treasurys ended little changed as evidence of weakness in housing was offset by a rebounding dollar, suggesting that foreign central banks may not need to buy as many U.S. bonds as part of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the yield on two-year \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds was 1.07%, up from 1.04% Wednesday. The yield on 10-year bonds also rose to 3.29% versus 3.24%, while the yield on 15-year bonds was at 3.72% against 3.66% previously.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year swap offer climbed to 1.46% against 1.43% late Wednesday, while the five-year offer was at 2.54%, compared with 2.48%. The 10-year swap rose to 3.63% from 3.58%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps was 217 basis points from 215 basis points previously.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank offer rate rose to 0.3125% from 0.1875%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet and Dawn Lye  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040219e02j000bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
