\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late, Sets Another 4-Year High Vs US Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 09:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar climbed to another new four-year high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in Asia Wednesday as offshore speculators and other players heavily sold the U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 At 0830 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6744, down from S$1.6758 late in Asia Tuesday. It traded a range of S$1.6686-S$1.6760 intraday. On Tuesday, the U.S. unit dropped to around S$1.6748, its lowest since late January 2000.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar selling was triggered by remarks from New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen early in the session that the government has "options" to halt the rise in the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Cullen's comments prompted speculators to unwind long Australian and New Zealand dollar positions against the Singapore dollar," said a Singapore dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Other speculators, many of whom were taking the Group of Seven's call for "more flexibility" from major nations or economic areas that "lack such flexibility" to heart, also joined in the U.S. dollar-selling spree against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said these speculators could be selling the U.S. dollar against the Singaporean and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a bet that the G7 wants Asian nations to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise in order to share more of the burden for the U.S. dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as low as S$1.6686 but later recovered to the mid-S$1.6700s on buying by Singapore banks, probably at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 After apparently staying out of the markets since the G7 meeting, the MAS is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to prevent the sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from hurting the city-state's exports.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS is likely to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a rapid appreciation in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair's short-term outlook remains bearish, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm looking at S$1.6620 then S$1.6500," said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds ended lower as they trailed the overnight retreat in U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 The overall tone in the domestic bond market was generally subdued ahead of testimony later Wednesday by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan before the House Financial Service Committee on the state of the U.S. economy and the outlook for monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 But the retreat in local bonds was somewhat tamed by the sharp rise of the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of Wednesday's session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds closed one basis point higher at 1.12%. At the longer end of the curve, the yield on the 10-year bonds rose six basis points to 3.31%, while the yield on 15-year bonds rose three basis points to 3.68%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest-rate swaps were also higher in line with local bond yields. The two-year swap offer was at 1.54% versus 1.51% late Tuesday, the five-year offer rose to 2.58% from 2.52%, while the 10-year swap gained to 3.64% from 3.58%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 200 basis points from 207 basis points Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank offer rate slipped to 0.6250% to 0.6875%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040211e02b000bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
