\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Surges Late To Fresh 4-Year High Vs US Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 09:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar surged to a fresh four-year high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in Asia Tuesday as broad-based U.S. dollar weakness returned to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with a vengeance.  \par \par \uc2 Offshore players, banks and companies sold the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair to either accumulate short positions or hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, driving the U.S. unit down to around S$1.6748, its lowest level since late January 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said a U.S. bank was rumored to have sold U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars in a "large" quantity out of Hong Kong, triggering stop-loss sales orders in the high-S$1.6700s and exacerbating the U.S. unit's slide against the Singaporean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6758, sharply lower from S$1.6851 late in Asia Monday. It traded a range of S$1.6748 to S$1.6844 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Rising sentiment that the Group of Seven's warning against "excess volatility" lack teeth and that the U.S. authorities will continue tolerating gradual U.S. dollar weakness prompted players to dump the U.S. unit against the Singaporean and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The near-term outlook for the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair continued to look bleak as there were no apparent signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ailing U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 While the MAS is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks to prevent a strengthening local dollar from hurting Singapore exports, this week's rise in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay out of the markets in order to allow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate in tandem, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. dollar could fall to S$1.6600-S$1.6700" soon, said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 According to technical charts, the U.S. dollar has also been in a downtrend against its Singaporean counterpart since Jan. 20.  \par \par \uc2 Technical analysis shows that the U.S. dollar triggered a bearish right triangle last Friday with its daily close below the horizontal lower edge of the pattern at S$1.6893 and that the downside target for the U.S. dollar is S$1.6638.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds were barely changed in very thin dealings Tuesday in line with the subdued activity in the overnight U.S. Treasurys market, which is awaiting Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony to Congress later this week.  \par \par \uc2 There could be some buying interest in local bonds if the Singapore dollar continues to strengthen, a trader said, noting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise to a new four-year high against the U.S. dollar late in the session.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, JP Morgan said the bullish trend in Singapore government bonds that started at the beginning of the year has ended. It expects a mild steepening in the bond curve and said Singapore's yield discount to Treasurys "still has potential to widen but will require higher U.S. rates first."  \par \par \uc2 At the end of Tuesday's trading, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was down at 1.10% from 1.13% Monday. The 10-year bond yield was slightly higher at 3.25% versus 3.23% previously, while the 15-year bond yield was at 3.65% against 3.64%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest-rate swaps were slightly lower. The two-year swap offer was at 1.51% versus 1.54% late Monday, the five-year offer fell to 2.52% from 2.55%, while the 10-year swap was flat at 3.58%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 207 basis points from 204 basis points Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank offer rate rose to 0.6875% from 0.6250%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040210e02a000db\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
