\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late; Test Of G7 Resolve Over Warning  \b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 09:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late Asia Monday, as speculators tested the resolve of the Group of Seven major industrialized nations to back its warning against "excess volatility" with action.  \par \par \uc2 At 0750 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6851, sharply down from S$1.6938 late in Asia Friday. It traded a range of S$1.6830 to S$1.6877 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially rebounded on short-covering following a new four-year low of S$1.6820 set late New York Friday, various players soon sold the U.S. unit amid sentiment the G7 might not jointly act against further U.S. dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "Market participants think (the G7 statement) will be only words and no action," said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 In a joint communique issued after their meeting in Boca Raton, Fla., over the weekend, the G7 finance officials added a new phrase warning that "excess volatility" and "disorderly movements" in exchange rates were "undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 Later in the session, however, the U.S. dollar's slide against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was slowed by rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  via domestic banks, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 While the G7's castigation of "excess volatility" may slow the U.S. dollar's slide somewhat in the short term, "(the statement) in and of itself is not strong enough to halt, let alone reverse it in the coming few months," said Bank of America in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 The G7's call for "more flexibility" from "major countries and economic areas that lack such flexibility" targets China and other non-Japan Asian nations and puts more diplomatic pressure on them to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, though rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation will continue to be resisted by central banks, Bank of America added.  \par \par \uc2 Given this international pressure on Asian countries to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen and the underlying U.S. dollar bearish sentiment, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could probe the psychologically key S$1.6800 level this week although the unit's downside is likely to be supported by MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds closed higher Monday following a gain in U.S. Treasurys Friday on weak U.S. jobs numbers.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. created 112,000 jobs in January, the fastest monthly rate for three years, but the figure was well below expectations of a 160,000 rise.  \par \par \uc2 Treasurys continued their ascent Monday in Tokyo, where traders viewed the G7 statement as positive for Treasurys because it didn't criticize Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a high expectation in the market that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds will be invested at the week's refunding tender for short-term (U.S. Treasury) notes," a trader said.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was down at 1.13% from 1.15% Friday. At the longer end of the curve, the yield on the 10-year bond was lower at 3.24% from 3.30%, while the 15-year bond yield was 3.64% from 3.70%.  \par \par \uc2 "Fears of bird flu was another factor helping Singapore bonds today; but with strong equity markets, the 10-year (government bond) should soon rise toward the 3.4%-3.5% region," a market strategist said.  \par \par \uc2 Interest-rate swaps were lower in line with local bond yields. The two-year swap offer was at 1.54% versus 1.57% late Friday, the five-year offer fell to 2.55% from 2.61%, while the 10-year swap fell to 3.58% from 3.66%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 204 basis points from 209 basis points Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank offer rate fell to 0.6250% from 0.7500% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040209e029000ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
