\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firm but wary of central banks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 10:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Jan 7 (Reuters) - Gains by Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were\par capped on Wednesday on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par the euro's retreat from a record high against the U.S. dollar.\par     The Singapore dollar        firmed but stayed off a fresh\par 44-month peak of 1.6951 per dollar it hit in offshore trade\par overnight. The South Korean won             was off a\par three-week high of 1,186.4 it touched on Tuesday.\par     The Taiwan dollar          rose to its strongest level\par since the middle of October at 33.756 on rising local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par demand ahead of the Chinese New Year, before losing some of\par these gains.\par     The Indonesian rupiah        was at 8,330, slightly off\par Tuesday's five-month high, while the Philippine peso\par traded within a narrow range shy of 55 per dollar, before\par falling to 55.245 at the close of local trade.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Thai baht        rose to test the 39.0 per dollar\par level, its highest since October, before dollar bids from some\par local banks restricted the rise.\par     Dealers in Singapore said similar dollar purchases by local\par or state-run banks in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Korea and talk of direct\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Taiwan prompted players to\par book profits on recent gains.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH\par     Dealers said investors and speculators would keep an eye on\par the euro  for fear that its retreat from Tuesday's record\par highs above $1.28 might prompt Asian central banks to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par even more aggressively.\par     "Central banks have been in the market and that is making\par players with large oversold U.S. dollar positions a bit\par nervous," said a dealer at a European bank in Singapore.\par     Analysts said if the central banks kept out, most regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would appreciate further on improved growth outlooks\par and large foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surpluses.\par     Bhanu Baweja, a strategist at UBS in Singapore, also said\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been pushed higher by foreign investors\par rebuilding positions in various asset classes across the\par region.\par  \par     "At this point with the start of the new year, people want\par to rebuild their positions in Asian markets," he said.\par     However, Baweja said Asian central banks were still worried\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 about the quality of these inflows and about the sustainability  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the economic recovery, especially in the second half of this  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 year when the risk is that the U.S. inventory build-up ends  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 without generating higher consumer demand and investment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Their big call is trying to figure out which of these  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 flows are transitory and which of these are long term," he  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders in Tokyo said they suspected the Bank of Japan had  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 repeatedly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since Monday to stop the yen  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 appreciating through the 106 per dollar level.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early on Wednesday, the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HK$4.27 billion to buy U.S. dollars and curb the Hong Kong  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong dollar traded at 7.7627/37, against the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 pegged level of HK$7.80 per dollar. Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 followed sales of HK$505 million on Monday and HK$4.06 billion  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day 0824 GMT   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.19         106.24       +0.05\par  Sing dlr        1.6966         1.6992       +0.15\par  Taiwan dlr      33.809         33.830       +0.06\par  Korean won     1186.80        1187.00       +0.02\par  Baht             39.01          39.16       +0.38\par  Peso             55.25          55.11       -0.24\par  Rupiah         8350.00        8330.00       -0.24\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.19         107.01       +0.77\par  Sing dlr        1.6966         1.7014       +0.28\par  Taiwan dlr      33.809         34.050       +0.71\par  Korean won     1186.80        1197.90       +0.94\par  Baht             39.01          39.59       +1.49\par  Peso             55.25          55.50       +0.46\par  Rupiah         8350.00        8460.00       +1.32.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040107e0170029h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\