\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late;Foreigners Buy, Local Bks Cap Gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 10:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar edged higher against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia Wednesday on some buying by foreign investment banks but its upside was curbed by light selling from domestic banks.  \par \par \uc2 Mounting U.S. dollar bearishness fueled buying of the Singaporean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by overseas speculators, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in small lots to prop up the U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 While the specter of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to continue to haunt players, and keep the local dollar from rising too sharply, the market forces behind the U.S. unit's slide could prove tough to fight, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the U.S. dollar set a fresh 47-month low of S$1.6955 late Tuesday in New York, as the U.S. unit weakened against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on concerns over the huge U.S. current account deficit and apparent indifference of U.S. monetary authorities toward the U.S. dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar is set to lose even more ground over the next few months, with analysts projecting it around S$1.6840 by the end of June.  \par \par \uc2 At 0910 GMT Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6979, down from S$1.6997 Tuesday in Singapore. Intraday, it traded between S$1.6962 and S$1.6986.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds rose early in the session on buying by market players to accumulate long positions, but mostly declined on profit-taking by some participants toward the session's close.  \par \par \uc2 The weakness of U.S. Treasurys in thin trading in Asia and worries about supply of new bonds from U.S. Treasury auctions this week also weighed on the prices of Singapore government bonds, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds may weaken in the coming sessions as market participants could take more profit on the bonds they bought earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the session, the yield on the two-year bonds had fallen one basis point to 1.05%. The yield on the 10-year bond had risen one basis point to 3.69%, while the 15-year bond yield moved up four basis points to 4.10%.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year interest rate swap was unchanged at 1.76%, while the five-year swap was offered three basis points higher at 3.06%. The 10-year offer ended four basis points higher at 4.16%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 230 basis points from 236 basis points late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In the local money market, rates were mostly steady, with the overnight interbank rate falling to 0.3125% from 0.3750% Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.haruidowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040107e017000bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
