\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strong start by Singapore dlr, Taiwan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2004\par 04:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Jan 2 (Reuters) - Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kicked\par off the first trading session of the year on a strong note on\par Friday, with the Singapore dollar close to 44-month highs and\par the Taiwan dollar at levels not seen since November.\par     The Taiwan dollar          rose to a high of 33.96 per U.S.\par dollar, a level last seen on November 11, before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap its rise, dealers said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar        held in a narrow range around\par the 1.6990 per U.S. dollar level, not far from the 44-month\par high of 1.6978 it hit in offshore trade on Wednesday.\par     Dealers in Singapore said the market was cautious amid\par fears the city-state's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Singapore, might step in to curb recent gains.\par     They said the Japanese yen's        retreat from the\par stronger side of the 107 per dollar level would discourage\par aggressive positioning.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "There are some (U.S.) dollar sellers in the market, but\par overall people are a bit cautious," said a dealer at a foreign\par bank.\par     Dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market gave a muted response to\par Singapore's advance estimate of fourth quarter gross domestic\par product growth of 3.7 percent year-on-year and 7.9 percent\par quarter-on-quarter annualised.\par     The seasonally adjusted annualised rate was slower than the\par third quarter's 17.3 percent growth, the fastest since 1997,\par and was weaker than the 11.2 percent rise most economists had\par expected in a Reuters survey.\par     Dealers said trading was dull in the region, with dealing\par rooms not fully staffed yet and with markets closed in Japan,\par Thailand and the Philippines.\par  \par     They said trading activity would be back to normal on\par Monday.\par     The South Korean won                   was a touch lower\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 from Wednesday's local close, but kept its distance from lows  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on the weaker side of the 1,200 per dollar level it hit earlier  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts expect most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strengthen further  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in 2004 on the momentum of global growth and a weak U.S. dollar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 environment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With strong GDP numbers expected in coming quarters and a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 soft U.S. dollar environment likely to persist, Asian  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be stronger," said David Simmonds, strategist  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 at the Royal Bank of Scotland in Singapore. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR Change on the day 0255 GMT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid\par Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       107.35         106.91       -0.41\par  Sing dlr        1.6993         1.7006       +0.08\par  Taiwan dlr      33.972         33.978       +0.02\par  Korean won     1195.80        1192.60       -0.27\par  Baht             39.63          39.62       -0.03\par  Peso             55.59          55.55       -0.07