\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Dn Late; US Dlr Rebounds From 39-Month Low  \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 09:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar rebounded smartly against its Singapore counterpart Thursday after hitting a 39-month low a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Buying by U.S. banks, broad-based U.S. dollar short-covering, and concern over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported the U.S. unit, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0820 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.7185, up from S$1.7127 late Wednesday in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's level late Thursday represented nearly a half-percent gain from its 39-month low of $1.7105 reached early Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The rebound in the U.S. dollar that started Wednesday was thought to be at least partly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , United Overseas Bank said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 "Now that MAS has been suspected to be in the market, it will probably make the market more wary of pushing dollar/Sing down too aggressively," according to UOB, which added that the U.S. dollar should find near-term support around the S$1.7100 level.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS doesn't comment on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but is believed to have sold Singapore dollars from time to time to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains from hurting demand for the country's exports.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar, helped by strong economic data, has been strengthening steadily since the start of November when the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7475.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of most Singapore government bonds fell after U.S. Treasurys slumped overnight amid concerns of new corporate issuance.  \par \par \uc2 Yields were up from 4-5 basis points across the curve early in the Singapore session before buying interest perked up later in the day and helped cut some losses.  \par \par \uc2 The yields on the 15- and 10-year Singapore government bonds, which were up as much as 5 basis points each in the morning, were just 1 basis point higher in late trading at 4.42% and 3.83% respectively.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the seven-year bond was 1 basis point lower at 3.26%, after trading as high as 3.31% in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the five-year bond was up 3 basis points at 2.87%, while the yield on the two-year bond was unchanged at 1.13%.  \par \par \uc2 In the interest rate swaps market, the two-year offer was up up 3 basis points at 1.84%. The five-year offer and seven-year offer were unchanged at 3.09% and 4.18% respectively.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 234 basis points from 237 basis points late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031120dzbk000eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
