\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; Dlr Up On US Data,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 12:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The dollar rose across the board against its Asian competitors Thursday, helped by an improving economic outlook in the U.S. and continued efforts by governments in the region to curb the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A better-than-expected index reading in the New York Federal Reserve Bank's October survey of manufacturers and positive retail sales data for September set a firm tone for the dollar in Asian trading.  \par \par \uc2 Central banks and government officials in the region have been taking advantage of the dollar's recent upward momentum to push their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower through regulation and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Given that G7 pressures to allow market forces to prevail (in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets) is really directed toward China and Japan, the remaining central banks have hidden behind the big boys and continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' competitiveness and hurt speculators," UBS said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won, in a volatile session, fell by more than 1% in the opening hour of trading as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to flush out short-dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 "A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -driven short squeeze in dollar/won continued for the third day in Asia today, pushing the pair back toward the KRW1,180 mark," UBS said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a 10-week high of KRW1,184.4 in morning trade before reversing course as interest in the local stock market perked up.  \par \par \uc2 The Korean Composite Stock Price Index rose 1.7% to finish at its highest level in a year. Foreign investors bought a net KRW89 billion worth of shares in the cash market and a net 8,548 futures contracts.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped to an intraday low of KRW1,117.2 late in the session. State banks acting on behalf of the government then stepped in to support the U.S. unit. That helped lift the dollar to its closing level of KRW1,174.9, up from KRW1,170.5 late Wednesday and its best finish since Sept. 2.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht was little changed as the initial shock from capital controls imposed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week began to wear off.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was quoted at THB39.850 late in Asia, about the same level as a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit rose to an intraday high of THB39.96 before profit-taking wiped out its gains.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said some players are starting to build short dollar positions again, hoping that the steep correction in the baht since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its new regulations Tuesday will allow the Thai unit to resume its appreciation on trade and investment capital flows.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped keep a lid on the local dollar by reiterating that the bank would not allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become a manipulation target in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.950, up from NT$33.900 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The New Taiwan dollar's rise to a 14-month high Monday fueled concerns that the surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could deal a setback to the country's export-reliant economy.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar tracked its regional peers lower in relatively calm trading after a string of recent volatile sessions.  \par \par \uc2 Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7410, up from S$1.7375 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit hit a session high of S$1.7450 in early trading before succumbing to profit-taking.  \par \par \uc2 With little in the way of domestic news to drive trading the Singapore dollar's moves were mainly dictated by the yen and the baht, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso also followed the yen lower after surrendering early gains.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at 54.66 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.62 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally over the past few sessions was cut short after the dollar's rise against the yen triggered a round of dollar/peso short-covering, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Indonesian rupiah, the dollar was quoted at IDR8,440 in late Asian trade, up from IDR8,425 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said players sold the rupiah in sympathy with the yen due to a lack of other news on which to trade.  \par \par \uc2 -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1136 4250  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031016dzag000no\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
