\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Central bank \highlight\uc2  moves bring big falls.  \b0\par\par\uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 12:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Several Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par posted their biggest losses in months on Tuesday, reversing\par most of the gains since the Group of Seven meeting last month\par that was seen as a call on Asian countries to let their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise.\par     Dealers said players scrambled to cover their short U.S.\par dollar positions as central banks, almost across the region,\par conducted aggressive dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The baht        fell about two percent to near 40 per\par dollar, a two-week low, after the Bank of Thailand placed fresh\par restrictions, including a limit on the amount of baht\par foreigners can keep in Thai bank accounts. The moves were aimed\par at curbing speculation in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, BOT said.\par     The Korean won            posted its biggest fall since\par March, to a near one-month low of 1,169.5, amid what dealers in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected was sporadic dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par the Bank of Korea.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted buying U.S. dollars to\par push its dollar  to a near one-month low on the weaker\par side of the 34 per U.S. dollar.\par     The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was also in the market to\par buy U.S. dollars and sell Hong Kong dollars , pushing the\par spot market to around 7.75 per dollar -- its closest to the\par official peg rate of 7.8 to the U.S. dollar since late\par September.\par  \par     The region-wide swing in sentiment pulled the Singapore\par dollar  to its lowest in more than three weeks.\par     "The move was aided by rumours of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 increasing concerns in the market about the post-G7 pace of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation, and overstretched short dollar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 positions," said Bank of America's strategist Sameer Goel.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the move was also partly in response to the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar's rise against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central  \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg said the G7 call last month for  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 exchange rate flexibility was not directed at the euro .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is still this issue of fear of floating where they  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 had been scared in the aftermath of the Asian crisis," said  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Claudio Piron, strategist at Standard Chartered commenting on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's steps to restrict capital flows.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "There is also an issue here of kind of protecting the\par constituency, the exporters."\par     Dealers said the move in the market has defied general\par bullish expectations ahead of President George W. Bush's visit\par to Asia next week and the APEC summit in Thailand.\par     Traders had expected Asian central banks to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less,\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 given United States' persistent criticism of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign  \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy of many Asian nations to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 weak.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     However, David Simmonds, strategist at Royal Bank of\par Scotland said the weak U.S. dollar trend was still intact.\par     "I think we are in a parameter shift here in terms of Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  going stronger against the U.S. dollar. That is the\par trend and I don't think that's altered," he said.\par     "Periodically we're going to get some pretty sharp and\par unpleasant corrections and this is one of them," he said.\par     Elsewhere, losses in the Philippine peso were limited by\par fresh dollar remittances from overseas workers.\par     Dealers said with the import season having peaked, and\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 overseas workers remitting dollars for the school admission  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 season, the peso was likely to see stronger levels in coming  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 days. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0914 GMT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     109.86       108.39         -1.34\par  Sing dlr      1.7398       1.7225         -0.99\par  Taiwan dlr    34.051       33.710         -1.00\par  Korean won   1166.40      1147.20         -1.65\par  Baht           39.99        39.20         -1.98\par  Peso           54.72        54.53         -0.35\par  Rupiah       8440.00      8370.00         -0.83\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency