\par\par \b \uc2 IMF/INTERVIEW:Singapore Econ Rebound Likely Underway-Min  \b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 04:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI (Dow Jones)--Singapore's economy is showing signs of recovery after a sharp contraction in the second quarter, but a top official declined to comment on whether the improvement will prompt a monetary policy shift.  \par \par \uc2 Second Finance Minister Lim Hng Kiang told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview that he is optimistic the recovery has begun as the world economy picks up and Singapore tackles its structural problems such as cutting business cost and diversifying the manufacturing base.  \par \par \uc2 "The economic recovery is taking place on a cyclical and structural level," Lim said on the sidelines of annual International Monetary Fund and the World Bank meetings here.  \par \par \uc2 In a separate interview, Lim was quoted as saying the economy will expand by 0.5%-0.7% this year, well within the government's twice downgraded zero to 1% growth forecast, the Singapore Business Times newspaper reported Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Lim's comments come as evidence emerge of an economic recovery in the U.S. and Asia - crucial export markets for Singapore whose trade volume is three times the S$160 billion gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 Sure signs of a turnaround from the second quarter's 11.4% on-quarter contraction when Singapore was hit by the SARS outbreak and the war in Iraq also suggests the government would be under less pressure to ease monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, where Lim is a deputy chairman, will early next month decide whether to shift the current neutral policy stance.  \par \par \uc2 In July, while the government retained the neutral stance, it re-centered the trade-weighted Singapore dollar at lower levels within the undisclosed band, effectively easing policy.  \par \par \uc2 Lim declined to be drawn into discussing the policy when directly asked whether further easing was a possibility, as several economists are predicting.  \par \par \uc2 "Our approach has always been to track the movement of a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that reflects our major trading partners," he said, referring to the long-standing government policy. "That has served us well in the past, as it allows flexibility and lets the market decide the value of the Singapore dollar," said Lim.  \par \par \uc2 In Singapore, where the impact of trade dominates a small domestic economy, the MAS conducts monetary policy via the exchange rate, rather than interest rates. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push up the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar when it wants to control inflation or an overheating economy and sells the local unit when the economy needs a boost.  \par \par \uc2 The authority guides the Singapore dollar in a narrow but undisclosed range - analysts estimate it is plus or minus 1% to 2% - against an unspecified trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Lim said economic weakness in the first half of the year was due both to weaker growth in the world economy, and new competitive challenges particular to Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "The weakness at the beginning of this year was partly cyclical and partly structural," he said. "Singapore faces a new competitive environment, and some sectors that had previously been doing well will have to be replaced by new value-added sectors," the minister added.  \par \par \uc2 -By Benjamin Pedley, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4044; ben.pedley@dowjones.com and Paul Hannon; +44-7776-200 927; paul.hannon@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hasan Jafri and Ryan Woo  \par \par \uc2 Dokument 