\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Soar On G7; Ctrl Bks Fail To Curb Gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Lim  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 941 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 11:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged Monday after Group of Seven finance ministers made an unprecedented call for increased flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to address global economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 Several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to multi-month highs against the U.S. dollar, while the forward discount on the dollar-China yuan pair steepened to record levels during the day.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 communique at the weekend didn't target any country, addressing its message to "major countries or economic areas."  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders immediately turned to the yen and other more freely-traded Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , interpreting that remark as confirming the end of Washington's "strong dollar" policy of the late 1990s and conversely, the start of stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The (G7) statement is a bit of a watershed. It explicitly said they want more flexibility in exchange rates," said BNP Paribas Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analyst Thio Chin Loo.  \par \par \uc2 "The market thinks it (G7) is pointing towards Asia where there is the greatest trade imbalances with the U.S.," Thio added.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, the dollar plunged to a 33-month low versus the yen in Asia Monday, and a 34-month low against the Korean won. The dollar was also sharply weaker against the Singapore dollar - at a near three-month low - and reached a 13-month low against the Taiwan dollar. Stock markets across the region, meanwhile, fell as investors fretted over what stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mean for corporate profits.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Korea Bought Dlrs; China Says Policy Stance Unchanged \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even jawboning from the Bank of Korea did nothing to stop dollar sales versus the won.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The recent trend in the market is not appropriate given Korea's economic fundamentals and we hope to see balance restored in the market on its own," said a high-ranking official at the Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea comments follow similar dollar-supportive remarks from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Economy earlier Monday. The ministry followed through on its verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by buying dollars via state-run banks, according to dealers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to as low as KRW1,150 intraday Monday, tracking the dollar-yen's sharp fall, and ended at a 34-month low of KRW1,151.2, versus Friday's close of KRW1,168. The yen-won cross was at 10.2620 late Monday, from Friday's 10.2501.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar sank in the market for forward delivery against the yuan, which can be used in place of the spot market to make bets on the yuan's long-term direction. The dollar's one-year discount steepened to a record 2400 points at one stage, implying expectations of the dollar falling to 8.0374 yuan over the next 12 months, a nearly 3% drop from the tight spot range that Beijing now allows around CNY8.277.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These expectations linger even though Chinese authorities insist nothing has changed post-G7.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's position on its current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime remains unchanged from the firm support for that policy expressed by People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan earlier this month, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spokeswoman said Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, a State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spokesman said, "We will continue to keep the renminbi (or yuan) exchange rate basically stable."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The NDFs imply the yuan is under intense pressure," BNP Paribas' Thio said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But China's (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) stance should stay for another one or two years," she added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Supplied NT$ \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.805, its lowest level since Aug. 14, 2002, versus Friday's close of NT$34.058. Dealings were valued at US$1.412 billion, the highest volume since May 30, 2002, compared with Friday's US$779 million.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Players in the non-deliverable forward market dumped the U.S. dollar, while the Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was "supplying Taiwan dollar unlimitedly" to absorb dollar offers, said a local bank trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar slid to as low as 1.7295 Singapore dollars, its lowest level in almost three months. But by the end of the local session, the dollar was well off its early lows due to concerns \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a possible easing of local monetary policy next month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United Overseas Bank said in a research report, "Over the next few days, U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar direction will be totally dependent on U.S. dollar-yen's outlook and whether the Bank of Japan will show up (to buy dollars)."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7322, down sharply from S$1.7490 late in Asia Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Thai baht, the dollar reached a 38-month low of THB39.93 and was at THB40.05 late Monday, versus late Friday's THB40.51. No Bank of Thailand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted though dealers said any baht sales by the authorities would likely have been easily absorbed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar ended at 55.07 pesos Monday, down from Friday's close of PHP55.165, largely in tandem with regional markets and the dollar-yen pair. The market shrugged off the Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's as-expected decision to keep rates unchanged.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian rupiah was barely traded as markets were shut for a religious holiday. Traders expect the rupiah to strengthen against the dollar Tuesday as the Indonesian unit catches up with the rises in its regional counterparts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Carolyn Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; carolyn.lim@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lim Mui Khi  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1136 4250  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030922dz9m000ir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
