\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late;USD/CNY NDF Discount Widens \b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 12:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar weakened against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, including a widening in the discount versus the Chinese yuan to a record level in the nondeliverable forwards market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar-yuan NDF discount has been steadily widening in recent months, reflecting a perception by market participants that China will eventually loosen the narrow trading band for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow it to appreciate against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The NDF market allows investors to make plays on potential moves in the dollar-yuan, despite the fact the yuan isn't freely traded. \par \par \uc2  The one-year dollar-yuan discount widened to an unprecedented 2,100 points Tuesday, just days ahead of The Group of Seven finance ministers meeting in which China's tight control over the yuan is expected to be an issue. \par \par \uc2  "There's just too much pressure from all over the world," a Hong Kong-based trader at a major U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise to a one-week high against the dollar in late Asian trading contributed to the widening of the dollar's discount versus the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Yen moves are catalysts for shifts in the yuan and Hong Kong dollar NDF markets because significant strength in the Japanese unit is viewed as placing pressure on those two fixed-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes. \par \par \uc2  Signs that the U.S. economic recovery may not be as robust as originally thought weighed on the dollar-yen. Late in Asian trading the dollar was quoted at Y116.40, down from Y117.50 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Asia, the South Korean won, New Taiwan dollar, Singapore dollar and Thai baht strengthened. The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso weakened. \par \par \uc2  The won was buoyed by the yen's strength, as well as foreign inflows into the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,170.0 won, down from KRW1,172.7 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners bought a net KRW37.3 billion worth of shares, helping push up the Korea Composite Stock Price Index by 1.3% on the day. \par \par \uc2  The won's gains were muted, however, by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar around the KRW1,170 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar overcame heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.103, down from NT$34.147 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at cushioning the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength accounted for between a third and half of Tuesday's total trading volume of US$605.5 million. \par \par \uc2  The peso weakened as demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to build in preparation for the quarter-end corporate dollar requirements. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at its intraday high of 55.05 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.965 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the dollar to gather more momentum during the week as importers and other companies build up their dollar holdings to pay for overseas purchases and settle debts. \par \par \uc2  Citigroup forecasts the dollar to reach PHP55.75 within a month due to a range of factors, including the lingering political concerns in the Philippines, the drain in dollar liquidity due to foreign debt payments, lower remittance inflows from migrant Filipino workers during the third quarter, and higher demand for dollars from importers. \par \par \uc2  With little in the way of news to drive trading, the Thai baht tracked other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of the Asian session the dollar was quoted at 40.510 baht, down from THB40.650 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Despite positive sentiment generated by the strength of Thai stocks and the economy, however, the baht failed to breach resistance around THB40.500. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local corporate demand supported the dollar, along with investors' wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7498 late in Asian trading, down from S$1.7521 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Demand for the Singapore dollar was tempered somewhat by a view that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  may want to keep a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to improve export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  In technical terms, analysts expect the local dollar to continue to move in a S$1.7480-S$1.7550 range after the U.S. unit failed to breach S$1.7670 key resistance recently. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah weakened slightly in an uneventful trading session. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at 8,465 rupiah, compared with IDR8,460 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local importers, mainly the state-owned oil and gas company Pertamina, began to buy dollars to meet month-end requirements. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030916dz9g000pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172