\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Helps Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 12:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The dollar was mixed against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, drawing some support from the Japanese government's efforts to prop up the U.S. unit against the strengthening yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought dollars for the third straight session, boosting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by nearly one full yen in early morning trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which was changing hands around Y116.80, soared to Y117.72 after the BOJ bought as much as $5 billion, according to some analysts' estimates. \par \par \uc2  The sharp rise against the yen triggered a round of dollar buying that boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the region. \par \par \uc2  But the yen battled back, supported by a strong stock market, improving economic numbers and expectations that Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi will be re-elected as leader of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party later this month. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar had slipped back below the Y117 threshold and was trading around Y116.97, up from Y116.83 from late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar backtracked against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the day progressed. The dollar finished slightly stronger against the South Korean won, New Taiwan dollar, and Philippine peso. It weakened against the Singapore dollar and Thai baht, and was unchanged against the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The won was pulled off its intraday lows with the help of foreign inflows into the local stock market and dollar sales by exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,171.5 won, up from KRW1,170.6 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net buyers of KRW142.7 billion worth of local shares Monday, although the size of their net purchases has shrunk from previous days. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also pressured by corporate sales ahead of a local holiday. \par \par \uc2  South Korean financial markets will be closed Wednesday through Friday for Chuseok, a thanksgiving holiday. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. unit closed at NT$34.129, up from NT$34.117 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar strengthened to NT$34.167 in the morning on the back of its gains against the yen, but lost ground in afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was helped by the recovering yen, as well as net inflows of NT$9.65 billion into the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar recovered from its early losses, helped by the selling of the U.S. dollar by foreign banks in afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7572, down from S$1.7580 late in Asia Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7580 in Singapore and after a quiet start drifted higher to touch its session high of S$1.7610 in tandem with its rise against the yen. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar failed to retain its gains as foreign banks unwound long U.S. dollar positions and took profit from midday. \par \par \uc2  The peso was pressured by lingering political and security concerns. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at its intraday high of 55.130 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP55.020 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The stability of the Philippine government has been called into question following corruption charges against President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo's husband, as well as a recent failed mutiny by some members of the military. \par \par \uc2  Traders said further peso weakening could be expected in the run-up to the May 2004 general election. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht finished stronger on the heels of the yen's late recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 40.73 baht, down slightly but little changed from THB40.775 at the Asian close Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the baht remains positive due to the strength of the local economy and local stocks. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian rupiah, the dollar was unchanged at 8,490 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Traders said expectations for further gains in the local stock market could boost the rupiah in the coming days. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta stocks ended at a three-and-a-half year high Monday, ending up 0.8% at 587.057 points. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Ryan Woo \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030908dz98000qr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
