\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late;Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Lifts Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 12:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The dollar strengthened against selected Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the U.S. unit against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been weakening against its Asian counterparts early in the session, including the yen, when the MOF stepped into the market. \par \par \uc2  The ministry is believed to have bought as much as $3 billion in the widely-rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The MOF, through the Bank of Japan, began buying dollars after speculative selling pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 15-week low of Y115.75 in the early hours of Asian trading. Within 20 minutes, the dollar jumped to Y116.90 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggered a wave of dollar short-covering. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-Asia was supported pretty much across the board this morning after the aggressive, unconfirmed, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan ...," Bank of America said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have worked to cap the yen's appreciation for fear excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength could jeopardize the country's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose sharply against all regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It held onto its gains against the New Taiwan dollar, Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah, but retreated later in the session against the South Korean won and Thai baht. The U.S. dollar was little changed against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar posted a fresh one-year intraday high in early trading before reversing course after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar traded as low as NT$34.030 in the morning session. It climbed steadily after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , finishing the day at NT$34.120, up from NT$34.095 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently took advantage of the U.S. dollar's momentum against the yen and jumped into the market to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  Many players who were short U.S. dollars were then forced to cover their positions, providing an even bigger boost to the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  succumbed to selling pressure against the won in afternoon trading as foreign investors continued to pour money into the South Korean stock market. Foreign investors bought a net KRW215.3 billion worth of shares Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Domestic corporations also unloaded the dollar in favor of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at KRW1,173.1, down from KRW1,174.3 at Wednesday's close. The dollar had changed hands as high as KRW1,176.5 after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The baht initially weakened in line with the yen, but later rallied to finish at a fresh 14-month high against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Fitch Ratings' upgrade of Thailand's sovereign ratings Wednesday continued to support a bullish view of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they expected the baht to continue its climb Friday. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 40.770 baht down from THB40.885 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Comments by the Thai prime minister published in the local press Thursday that the dollar isn't likely to fall beyond THB39 gave investors the impression that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market until the pair gets closer to that level, a Bangkok-based trader said. \par \par \uc2  The peso snapped a three-day winning streak as it tracked the yen lower and gave way to corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded from an intraday low of PHP54.620 to finish Asian trading at PHP54.950, up from PHP54.755 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Corporations bought dollars as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's losses earlier this week have brought it down to levels that are perceived to be attractive for debt servicing purposes. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was supported by persistent buying interest from some market participants despite a sharp fall in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7558, compared with S$1.7562 late in Asia Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian rupiah, the dollar was quoted at IDR8,480 rupiah in late Asian trading compared with IDR8,475 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand from local importers drove the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to an intraday high of IDR8,495 before inflows into the local stock market pulled the unit lower. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited 