\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS-Tokyo stocks fall, Japan  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Baum  \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 08:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 4 (Reuters) - Tokyo shares fell for the first time in five sessions on Thursday on worries stocks had risen too fast and the yen tumbled as Japan apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect an economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  The jitters in Japan were mirrored in South Korea and Taiwan. But shares in Hong Kong and Australia scored 14-month highs on signs of an improving global economy, helping to lift an MSCI index of Asia Pacific stocks ex-Japan 0.5 percent to levels unseen since February 2001.   \par \par \uc2  "You're starting to see signs of recovery in most major economies - China, Japan, the U.S. is starting to turn around, in Europe there's a flicker of light at the end of the tunnel," said Mick Coombes, director at Australia's Macquarie Equities.   \par \par \uc2  Oil and gold prices were little changed, but Japanese government bonds rallied for a second day after the Bank of Japan vowed to stick to its ultra-loose monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 10-year bond tumbled 14 basis points to 1.475 percent.   \par \par \uc2  In another move intended to keep a budding economic recovery on track, the Bank of Japan was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem export-damaging strength in the yen, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  They said yen sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than one percent off multi-month highs versus the dollar and the euro.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot up more than one yen to 116.84 yen from a 3-1/2-month low of 115.75 hit in early trade. The euro rose about 1.5 yen from the day's low to 126.60 yen after hitting an eight-month low of 125.05 yen on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  ECONOMY OFF SICK BED   \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese economy is like a bedridden man who has finally recovered but has only just started to walk a little bit," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp. "It's clear the government doesn't want to kill the nascent recovery."   \par \par \uc2  The yen has been rising on foreign demand for Japanese stocks, encouraged by indicators such as a capital spending report on Thursday that suggest the economy is improving.   \par \par \uc2  Although laggards in the recent rally such as drug makers continued to gain, concern that stocks were overheating pulled the Nikkei average lower. The market had gained more than 15 percent in less than a month to hit 14-month highs.   \par \par \uc2  "We seem to be losing some of our momentum after the market rocketed higher earlier this week," said Norihiro Fujito, senior strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.   \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei closed down 0.6 percent at 10,646.95 as top technology stocks fell. Tokyo Electron dropped 4.4 percent and Toshiba Corp fell 3.3 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Top drug maker Takeda Chemical Industries Ltd rose 3.7 percent and rival Sankyo Co Ltd surged 8.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2  South Korea and Taiwan also fell prey to worries stocks had risen too far on hopes a U.S. economic recovery would boost exports from Asia - their benchmark indices fell 0.3 percent and 0.8 percent respectively.   \par \par \uc2  But Hong Kong stocks reached 14-month peaks, with the Hang Seng Index ending morning trade up 0.6 percent at 11,171.68. Australian shares hit the same milestone, climbing half a percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030904dz94000mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
