\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Some Ctrl Bks Help Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2003\par 12:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The yen's strength and portfolio flows into regional stock markets lifted most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, with the South Korean won ending at its strongest level in more than a year and the New Taiwan dollar chalking up its best finish in more than 11 months. \par \par \uc2  Monetary authorities in several countries were suspected of buying U.S. dollars to temper their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains, but the broad trend remained intact. \par \par \uc2  Policymakers in the region are unwilling to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen too much for fear of losing out on export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was a notable exception to the general trend as investors bought the U.S. unit after it slid to a two-month low in the overnight global session. \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7454, up from $1.7398 in Asia late Thursday and S$1.7330 overnight. \par \par \uc2  A foreign bank dealer said local banks were supporting the U.S. dollar. He wasn't sure if they were acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore but "I wouldn't be surprised," he said. \par \par \uc2  But an Asian bank dealer said offshore banks were the main buyers of U.S. dollars when it staged a rebound around mid-afternoon. \par \par \uc2  A research report by JP Morgan Chase calling for investors to sell the Singapore dollar in favor of the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against which the MAS guides the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have contributed to the move, he said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS guides the local dollar within an undisclosed trading band against the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trading partners. It occasionally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to iron out excess volatility and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar within the band. \par \par \uc2  As "the SGD (Singapore dollar) is hitting the upper limit against its trade-weighted index, concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the SGD's rise will help support the USD," BNP Paribas said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  "Nonetheless, it should not alter the bullish trend for the SGD, which has leeway to appreciate further as the JPY and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue their rise," the French bank said. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the dollar ended at the intraday low of 1,169.5 won - its lowest close since July 2002 - and down from Thursday's KRW1,173.0. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit extended losses toward the close despite steady dollar purchases likely on behalf of the government. The state-run Korea Development Bank was buying the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market was deluged with dollars by foreign stock investors, but investors (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) were getting cannier about more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the government, which had already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at every level today," said Seo Jiwang, a dealer at State Street. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors, who were net buyers of more than KRW1 trillion in shares on the Korea Stock Exchange this week, are selling dollar for won to settle purchases. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect dollar sales to continue into Monday, possibly leading the dollar as low as KRW1,165. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities won't let the dollar/won free fall, but I think it's heading downward anyway," said a dealer at Kookmin Bank. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. unit ended at NT$34.217, its lowest since ending at NT$34.122 last Sept. 5, and down from NT$34.238 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said strong inflows from overseas investors to invest in Taiwan's equity markets lifted the local dollar. Foreign investors bought a net NT$7.20 billion in shares Friday, according to the Taiwan Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume totaled US$1.02 billion, up from US$731 million in the previous session and the heaviest in over a month. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local dollar from rising further accounted for a big portion of the volume, a Taipei dealer said. Another dealer estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought around US$600 million in the session. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem sharp movements in the New Taiwan dollar, and to keep it from strengthening too much for fear it might hurt exports. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was lower as investors, mainly offshore, unloaded the dollar, though agents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were spotted buying the U.S. unit, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at 41.50 baht, down from THB41.55 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A bank trader in Singapore reported heavy dollar sales at the THB41.48-THB41.50 level earlier in the session, but "those levels will be supported by Bank of Thailand." \par \par \uc2  He said, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be trying to slow the baht's rise, rather than reverse the trend. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was lower in subdued trading as traders covered short-dollar positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,425 rupiah, up from IDR8,390 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine markets were closed for a holiday Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030822dz8m000ni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
