\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Weaker Late; Selling Led By Foreign Banks \b0\par\par\uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2003\par 10:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar weakened Friday on selling led by foreign banks in a very thin illiquid market. \par \par \uc2  At 0820 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7454, up from S$1.7398 late in Asia Thursday. It traded between S$1.7390 and S$1.7467 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7392, near its session low, and at first couldn't establish a clear course after rising strongly against the euro but falling against the yen in New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar held steady for much of the morning session as market participants were concerned the sharp overnight fall would prompt central banks in the region, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar rebounded sharply around midafternoon as foreign banks aggressively bought the U.S. dollar from around S$1.7400, pushing it up to S$1.7467. The move may have been somewhat exaggerated by thin liquidity, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The offshore banks were the main buyers. But it's pretty much the Sing (Singapore dollar) all on its own. All the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are generally steady," an Asian bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A research report by JPMorgan Chase Bank calling for investors to sell the Singapore dollar in favor of the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against which the MAS guides it may have also contributed to the moves, he added. \par \par \uc2  The MAS guides the Singapore dollar within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. It occasionally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to iron out excess volatility and to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the undisclosed band. \par \par \uc2  Although there were some concerns that the MAS may have played a role in the U.S. dollar's afternoon rebound Friday, there seemed to be little evidence to support that, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Although the U.S. dollar was off its intraday high by the end of the Asian session, dealers said further gains were possible in the global trading day, though "I think it should be capped at S$1.7500," another trader said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were higher Friday, after recovering from their early falls, thanks to bargain hunters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds opened lower after sharp losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight. But bonds rebounded around midday, said market participants, on continued relief after the MAS announced a smaller-than-expected issue size of new 15-year bonds due Sept. 1. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said Wednesday that it would auction S$1.5 billion in new 15-year bonds Aug. 27, smaller than market expectations of between S$2 billion and S$2.5 billion, easing concerns that the market would be flooded with new issues. \par \par \uc2  At the end of Friday's session, the yield on two-year bonds was down two basis points at 1.38%, while the yield on five-year bonds shed three basis points to 2.51%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was also three basis points lower at 3.18%, while the yield on 10-year bonds fell four basis points to 3.56%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was one basis point lower at 3.77%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, offers were also mostly lower, tracking local bond yields. The two-year offer, bucked the trend though, holding steady at 1.55%, while the five-year swap offer slipped one basis point to 2.76%. The 10-year offer was five basis points lower at 3.77%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 222 basis points from late Thursday's 227 basis points. \par \par \uc2  In the local money market, rates were steady as liquidity remained sufficient in the banking system. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.8125% 0.8750%   0.8125% 0.8750%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            0.9375% 1.0625%   0.9375% 1.0625%\par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Ryan Woo\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030822dz8m000jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
